<div class="section-eyebrow">The project</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">What Thailand is proposing, and where it stands.</h2>

<p class="prose">The Kra Isthmus is the narrow neck of the Thai peninsula. Thailand has proposed a canal or land crossing here on and off since the 19th century. The current plan is a land bridge connecting a deepwater port at Chumphon on the Gulf of Thailand to one at Ranong on the Andaman Sea, joined by roughly 90 kilometres of dual-track rail and a motorway. It is costed at about 997.68 billion baht, roughly $28–29 billion, and structured as a 50-year public-private concession under a single private operator.</p>

<p class="prose">Promoters argue it could cut transit times by up to four days and shipping costs by around 15 percent for cargo that would otherwise sail around Singapore. As of mid-2026 the project was under a 90-day review announced by Thailand's finance minister, with bidding and construction targeted for 2026 and a first phase for 2030 in earlier official timelines. Independent reporting notes that no major shipping line has committed to use it. Treat the timeline as a promoter's schedule, not a forecast.</p>

<p class="prose">The Hormuz closure that began on 28 February 2026 gave the proposal fresh geopolitical momentum. If any single corridor can be closed, the argument goes, every alternative becomes more valuable. That is true. What it leaves open is whether this particular alternative can carry the cargo that makes Malacca a risk problem in the first place.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">What this analysis does and does not claim</div>
  <div class="callout-body">We are not forecasting whether the land bridge gets built, and we are not making a call on its merits as sovereign infrastructure. We are asking one narrower question: <strong>for the commodities that actually concentrate dependency on Malacca in our model, does a double-handled land crossing change anything?</strong> The cost figures below use stated, substitutable assumptions. They are illustrations of the economics, not quotes.</div>
</div>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<div class="section-eyebrow">The core constraint</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">A land bridge is not a canal. Every tonne has to come off one ship and onto another.</h2>

<p class="prose">A canal lets a vessel sail through. A land bridge does not. Cargo has to be discharged at one port, moved across the isthmus, and loaded onto a second vessel. For containers that is expensive but routine, because containers are built to be lifted. For bulk cargo it is a different kind of operation.</p>

<p class="prose">Dry bulk handling at a modern terminal typically costs around $8–12 per tonne for discharge, storage and reloading. A Capesize carrying about 170,000 tonnes of iron ore would therefore pay roughly <strong>$1.7 million in handling alone</strong> at $10 per tonne, before rail freight, port dues or the extra port-call days. The alternative, a four-day detour via Lombok, costs about $200,000 at the assumptions below (four days at roughly $32,000 bunkers plus $18,000 charter per day). On those numbers handling would have to fall to about $1.20 per tonne, roughly a tenth of typical terminal rates, before the land crossing matched a short sea detour.</p>

<p class="prose">Liquid bulk is harder again. A VLCC carries roughly 270,000 tonnes of crude. Moving that through shore tanks and onto a second tanker needs dedicated crude terminals, with handling at perhaps $15–20 per tonne plus evaporation and contamination management. We found no crude or LNG transshipment capacity in the published project design, which is described around container ports, rail and motorway. That is the main reason we treat crude and LNG as outside what the project is built to carry. It is a statement about the published design, not a proof that no one could ever build such terminals.</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<div class="section-eyebrow">The rerouting economics</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Land bridge versus the sea detour, by vessel class.</h2>

<p class="prose">The Narrows model costs a reroute from three inputs: charter rate, bunker consumption and detour days. For the land bridge it adds double-handling cost per tonne (or per TEU) and an estimated 1.5 days of additional port time at each end. The cards below compare the two for a laden vessel on a representative Asia route.</p>

