<p class="prose">Containers are the only class where the project's own design points at the cargo. Even there, for ultra-large container ships the double-handling bill runs several times the cost of a short detour, which is why the plausible users are smaller feeder vessels and time-sensitive cargo where days saved carry real value.</p>

<div class="callout-verdict">
  <div class="callout-verdict-label">The core finding</div>
  <div class="callout-verdict-body">On the assumptions stated in the methodology note, <strong>a double-handled land crossing costs more than the short sea detour it would replace for dry bulk,</strong> and the published design contains no crude or LNG capacity at all. The cargo the project is built for, containers, is a real use case, but it is not where Malacca's dependency concentrates in our model.</div>
</div>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<div class="section-eyebrow">The dependency matrix</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Where Malacca exposure actually sits: crude, refined products and gas, not iron ore.</h2>

<p class="prose">A common framing of the Kra case is that Japanese and Korean steelmakers need a Malacca alternative for iron ore. The data does not support that. In CDM v3, iron ore bound for Japan and South Korea is routed around Malacca already: Lombok carries 98.9 percent of Japan's iron ore and 98.7 percent of Korea's, so their Malacca dependency for that commodity is 0.8 percent each. The Malacca exposure for those economies is in energy: crude petroleum at 95.7 percent for Japan and 75.4 percent for South Korea, refined products for Korea at 81.8 percent, and LNG at 31.1 percent and 48.9 percent respectively. China's crude dependency is lower, at 46.2 percent, but on a much larger base at $151.1 billion a year.</p>

